This is a quick logistical question from someone very new to the =
distance sampling world. I am currently using both point and line =
transects to monitor carnivore prey levels in the Serengeti, Tanzania. =
On the whole this is a perfect environment for distance sampling with =
most species in distinct groups and clear fields of view for relatively =
easy distance estimation. However, when the migration moves through, the =
entire methodology breaks down - clusters are no longer distinct and =
therefore there is no easily definable centre-of-cluster to estimate a =
distance to. The animals effectively form one giant cluster which =
usually extends out of sight. At present, the best I can do is simply =
estimate numbers of animals within certain distance bands, which means =
my methodology changes according to prey concentration, which is not =
exactly ideal. Does anyone have experience with such variable counting =

conditions? If so I would greatly appreciate any advice.

Tom Maddox
Serengeti Cheetah Project student

