From ua52219e2e622d1af at groups.google.com  Tue Jan 25 09:17:52 2000
From: ua52219e2e622d1af at groups.google.com (Steve Buckland)
Date: Tue, 25 Jan 2000 09:17:52 +0000
Subject: Re: cluster estimation
Message-ID: <TDY3-50Q5do.4F44wWiEIW0J@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <TDY3-50Q5do.0Jv7i3_hocIJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
References: <TDY3-50Q5do.0Jv7i3_hocIJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/distance-sampling/c/TDY3-50Q5do/m/4F44wWiEIW0J>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

> > This topic is another good reason for Len to include some strip sampling
> > components in a future edition of Distance....(sorry Len!)

Distance can already do strip transects.  Select the uniform key.  For
each animal counted, you can put an arbitrary detection distance -
anything between 0 and w (the strip half-width) will do.  Then in the data
filter, define a single interval for the data, running from 0 to
w.  Distance will then give you a strip transect analysis.

We should perhaps make this more obvious, with a strip transect option,
and no requirement to enter meaningless distances.  A finite popn
correction option might also be of use for strip transects, for when a
high proportion of the study area is covered and the objects are
stationary (e.g. plant or dung surveys).

Steve

